🎯 The Big Picture — 5 Categories

Think of status codes like traffic signals with a first digit that tells you the VIBE immediately:

Starts with	Meaning	Vibe
1xx	Informational	"Hold on, processing..." 🤔
2xx	Success ✅	"All good, here you go!" 😊
3xx	Redirection ↪️	"It moved, go over there instead" 🚶
4xx	Client Error 🙅	"YOU messed up the request"
5xx	Server Error 💥	"WE messed up, not your fault"

Golden rule: 4xx = your problem, 5xx = server's problem. Never mix these up in an interview 😅

✅ 2xx — Success Family
200 OK 👍

"Here's exactly what you asked for."

The most common one. GET request worked, here's your data.

201 Created 🎉

"New thing successfully created!"

Used after a successful POST — you asked to create something (a new user, new post), and it worked.

POST /users → 201 Created (new user was made!)
GET /users/101 → 200 OK (just fetched existing data)

Notice: POST that creates something = 201, not 200! Common interview gotcha. 😉

↪️ 3xx — Redirection Family
301 Moved Permanently 📦

"This address doesn't exist anymore, we PERMANENTLY moved. Update your bookmarks!"

Used when a URL has permanently changed (like your gf changing her house address forever — stop mailing the old one 😂).

yoursite.com/old-page → 301 → yoursite.com/new-page

Browsers/search engines remember this and automatically use the new URL next time.

🙅 4xx — Client Error Family (YOUR fault)
400 Bad Request ❌

"Bro what even IS this request? I can't even understand it."

You sent malformed data — broken JSON, missing required fields, wrong data type.

POST /users  Body: { name: }  ← broken syntax → 400
401 Unauthorized 🔐

"Who ARE you? I don't even know you. Log in first."

You're NOT authenticated — no valid login/token provided at all.

403 Forbidden 🚫

"I know EXACTLY who you are... but you're still not allowed in here."

You ARE authenticated (server knows who you are), but you don't have PERMISSION for this specific action.

401 vs 403 — the classic confusion:

	401	403
Meaning	"I don't know you"	"I know you, but you're not allowed"
Analogy	Trying to enter a club without an ID	Showing your ID, but you're not on the VIP guest list
404 Not Found 🕳️

"Whatever you're looking for... doesn't exist here."

The single most famous error on the internet. URL/resource simply doesn't exist.

💥 5xx — Server Error Family (SERVER's fault)
500 Internal Server Error 💣

"Something broke on OUR end. It's not you, it's us."

Generic "the server crashed/had a bug while processing your request." You did nothing wrong — the backend code itself failed.